# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEOS Trade Agent - line ending policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `npm run build` generates `public/` from `src/dashboard/public/`. For the
# generated file to be reproducible, the source it is generated from has to
# have the same line endings on every machine and on every CI runner. Without a
# policy here, git decides that per developer via `core.autocrlf`, so a Windows
# checkout gets CRLF, a Linux checkout (including Vercel's build) gets LF, and
# the same commit produces two different `public/index.html` files.
#
# That is not hypothetical. `scripts/build.js` originally inserted two lines
# using a hard-coded `\n` while the surrounding file was CRLF, producing a file
# with 141 CRLF and 2 bare LF. It passed every test in the repository, because
# the drift checks byte-compared only the *verbatim* copies and never the
# *patched* file. It was found in a second clone on a different filesystem.
#
# The scope below is deliberately narrow. Only the paths the build generates
# from, and the build output itself, are pinned. The rest of the repository is
# left alone, because normalising every tracked file would bury the real change
# in a repository-wide diff and touch code that has nothing to do with the
# deployment.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree form as well as the repository form, so a
# Windows developer checking these paths out gets LF and produces byte-identical
# output to a Linux CI runner.

/src/dashboard/public/**  text eol=lf
/src/vercel/public/**     text eol=lf
/public/**                text eol=lf